Zimmermann PWA Kit, SFCC-236 product card, PR #85 CodeRabbit rework. One confirmed, verified finding to fix — a real gap in a negative test assertion, not a functional/production code change.

File: `overrides/app/components/ui-kits/organisms/zimmProductCard/index.test.tsx`

Context: the test immediately above this one (starting around line 900, `'an ancestor :has(:focus-visible) + the real recipe's own descendant-selector suffix flips from non-matching to matching once a nested control receives real focus'`) was already fixed earlier this session to use `await user.tab()` instead of a bare `.focus()` call, because this project's jsdom+nwsapi has a confirmed quirk: a bare `.focus()` call only satisfies `:focus-visible` when nothing else in the same test FILE has already driven a real pointer-originated focus via `userEvent.click()` (Vitest's jsdom environment is created once per file, not per test, so state carries over). That test's own comment (lines 923-937) documents this in full, with the empirical confirmation.

The very next test in the same describe block — the negative guard, `'the reveal selector still does not match a sibling control receiving focus outside the card root entirely'` — was never updated during that rework and still uses a bare `.focus()` call. Given this file has many earlier `userEvent.click()` calls in other describe blocks (e.g. the "every interactive control stays separately clickable" block), by the time this test runs, `:focus-visible` likely never engages at all for this bare `.focus()` call — meaning `expect(sizeChips.matches(revealSelector)).toBe(false)` passes vacuously (nothing has `:focus-visible`, not because the selector genuinely excludes outside-card focus). CodeRabbit flagged this independently and correctly on the open PR; verified as a real, valid finding, not a false positive.

Current exact code (read the file first to confirm this still matches before editing):
```tsx
  test('the reveal selector still does not match a sibling control receiving focus outside the card root entirely', () => {
    const ANCESTOR_STAND_IN = '[data-testid="card-root"]';
    const revealSelector = FOCUS_VISIBLE_SIZE_CHIPS_KEY.replace('&', ANCESTOR_STAND_IN);

    const { container } = render(
      <>
        <button type="button">Outside the card</button>
        <div data-testid="card-root">
          <div data-card-slot="content">
            <div data-card-slot="sizeChips">size chips</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </>,
    );

    const sizeChips = container.querySelector(`[data-card-slot="${CARD_SLOT.sizeChips}"]`) as HTMLElement;
    screen.getByRole('button', { name: 'Outside the card' }).focus();

    // Guards against a selector so loose it would match regardless of where focus actually is —
    // focus outside the card root must never trigger the reveal.
    expect(sizeChips.matches(revealSelector)).toBe(false);
  });
```

Change to exactly this (mirroring the sibling test's own established pattern — `userEvent.setup()`, `await user.tab()`, and an explicit intermediate assertion that focus actually landed where expected before asserting the real thing under test):

```tsx
  test('the reveal selector still does not match a sibling control receiving focus outside the card root entirely', async () => {
    const user = userEvent.setup();
    const ANCESTOR_STAND_IN = '[data-testid="card-root"]';
    const revealSelector = FOCUS_VISIBLE_SIZE_CHIPS_KEY.replace('&', ANCESTOR_STAND_IN);

    const { container } = render(
      <>
        <button type="button">Outside the card</button>
        <div data-testid="card-root">
          <div data-card-slot="content">
            <div data-card-slot="sizeChips">size chips</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </>,
    );

    const sizeChips = container.querySelector(`[data-card-slot="${CARD_SLOT.sizeChips}"]`) as HTMLElement;
    // Keyboard-originated focus via user.tab(), not a bare .focus() call — see the sibling test
    // above (lines ~923-937) for the full jsdom/nwsapi quirk this sidesteps: a bare .focus() call
    // only satisfies :focus-visible when nothing earlier in this test file has already driven a
    // real userEvent.click()-originated focus, which this file's own earlier describe blocks do.
    // Without this, the assertion below could pass vacuously — false because nothing has
    // :focus-visible at all by this point in the run, not because the selector genuinely excludes
    // focus outside the card root. The intermediate assertion confirms focus actually landed on
    // this element before the real thing under test is checked.
    await user.tab();
    expect(screen.getByRole('button', { name: 'Outside the card' })).toHaveFocus();

    // Guards against a selector so loose it would match regardless of where focus actually is —
    // focus outside the card root must never trigger the reveal.
    expect(sizeChips.matches(revealSelector)).toBe(false);
  });
```

Confirm `userEvent` is already imported at the top of this file (it must be, since the sibling test above already calls `userEvent.setup()`) — do not add a new import if it's already there; only report if it's somehow missing (it shouldn't be).

After the edit, run:
- `npx tsc --noEmit`
- `npx vitest run overrides/app/components/ui-kits/organisms/zimmProductCard/index.test.tsx` — run it at least twice in a row (or as part of the full file, not in isolation) to confirm this fix is robust to the same test-order sensitivity the sibling test's own comment describes, not just passing when run alone.
- `npm run test -- --run` (full suite) once, to confirm nothing else regresses.

All must be clean/green. Do not touch any other test in this file, and do not touch any non-test file — this is a single-test, test-only change. Do not run `git add` or commit. Report back exactly what changed and the full tsc/vitest output.

